<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-distribution-stories> {
  ex:l5-n09-story1-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n09-story1-heading, ex:l5-n09-story1-plot ; cd:narration "Our investigation asks about mean nitrate concentration in a defined water body at a fixed survey time. We cannot analyse its entire volume, so we need an environmental sampling design and an analytical procedure. Start by inspecting the laboratory part: one collected homogeneous sample is measured five times by UV/Vis. The constructed values 9.6, 9.8, 10.0, 10.2 and 10.4 mg/L have mean 10.0 mg/L. The mean marker is not a sixth observation. These are laboratory repeats, not five independently selected environmental samples. They teach us about analytical variation within one sample, not whether that sample represents the water body. The next slide places this laboratory variation inside the full investigation before introducing a model for it."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n09-story2-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n09-story2-heading, ex:l5-n09-story2-list ; cd:narration "The target is the volume-weighted mean nitrate concentration in a defined water body at one fixed survey time, not the next measurement result. A heterogeneous water body need not have the same concentration everywhere. We must connect selected water units to that target through an appropriate sampling design. Laboratory repeats then help characterize analytical variation in each collected sample. They cannot establish environmental representativeness or remove selection bias. For teaching we first assume normal measurement errors, with specified analytical mean 10.0 mg/L and SD 0.30 mg/L, not an SD inferred from these five points. This is only the analytical component, not a model of the water body's concentration distribution. Later we use a separate, explicitly idealized environmental sampling simulation to study the mean estimator. Specified parameters help explain how estimates vary; an actual investigation must estimate unknown quantities from its sample and justify both sampling and analytical assumptions."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-l12-story-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:lognormal-concentration-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-l12-story-heading, ex:l5-l12-story-plot ; cd:narration "The site comparison showed why we cannot transfer the normal measurement-error model unchanged to environmental variation. Keep nitrate as the analyte, but now compare water samples with different transport and dilution histories. More dilution reduces a concentration by a factor; less dilution can leave a concentration larger relative to a comparison sample. Illustrate relative changes around 2 mg/L: half is 1, unchanged is 2, double is 4. Reciprocal factors do not produce equal-sized additive changes on the concentration scale. These are deterministic scenarios, not sampled sites, a random simulation or evidence of a distribution. Variable multiplicative influences motivate examining a logarithmic representation, not automatically assuming a lognormal law. The next slide introduces that candidate with twenty separately constructed values, not data collected at these sites or inferred from the three scenarios. We will keep those twenty values while moving between the concentration and log-ratio scales. The specified model median is 2 mg/L; returning to the concentration scale will explain why its arithmetic expectation is different."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-variance-story-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16-variance-story-heading, ex:l5-t16-variance-story-plot ; cd:narration "Our uncertainty assessment uses an estimated spread, but that estimate itself varies. Stay with the laboratory component: each five-measurement precision series estimates analytical variance. The bars reuse the first five variance estimates of S15's normal simulation in order, not selected extremes or measured laboratory evidence. Each bar is one series summary, not one environmental unit. A changed estimated variance alone does not prove an unstable method. Under iid normal sampling, scaling the sample variance gives a chi-square reference. The next slides explain that reference and its degrees of freedom, preparing the reason a mean interval changes when SD is estimated. This laboratory reference must not be transferred unchanged to a nonnormal environmental population."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-mean-story-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16-mean-story-heading, ex:l5-t16-mean-story-plot ; cd:narration "Return to the five laboratory repeats of the collected nitrate sample, with mean 10.0 mg/L. The population SD is not supplied as known, so we estimate it from the same small series. Both mean and estimated spread would change in a repeated laboratory study. We want an interval for the common analytical expectation under an explicit iid normal model. This is only the laboratory component, not the environmental mean interval and not evidence that one sample represents the water body. First contrast genuinely known SD with estimated SD; Student-t then accounts for the random estimated denominator. Its worked interval will use these exact five laboratory values. Normality, independence and absence of bias are assumptions, not conclusions from the constructed points. The four environmental lognormal draws remain a separate dataset and do not acquire an exact t interval from this example."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-f17-story-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-f17-story-heading, ex:l5-f17-story-plot ; cd:narration "Analytical method choice also affects the precision of sample results used in our investigation. Two nitrate methods have constructed sample SDs 0.20 mg/L for A and 0.40 mg/L for B. These summaries concern laboratory repeatability, not selection of representative environmental units. The larger observed spread does not prove a population precision difference, accuracy or method superiority. Sample sizes and raw measurements are not supplied; we do not invent them. Squaring the SDs gives a descriptive variance ratio four. Because both variances are estimated, their ratio also varies, motivating an F reference under independent normal-sample equal-variance assumptions. The illustrative F parameters are not missing sample sizes inferred from these summaries. A formal test decision remains deferred. This component can inform analytical uncertainty, but it cannot validate the environmental sampling design."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .

